GREP(1)                        P32W Unix                         GREP(1)

NAME
       grep - search files for lines matching a pattern

SYNOPSIS
       grep [-vnci] [--] pattern [file ...]

DESCRIPTION
       grep reads each file, or the standard input if none is named,
       and prints the lines that contain a match for pattern. When
       more than one file is named, each line printed is preceded by
       its file name and a colon. Lines may be of any length.

       The pattern is a regular expression:

       c      an ordinary character matches itself
       \c     matches c literally (for . * [ ^ $ and \)
       .      any character
       [...]  any character in the set; a-z is a range; a ]
              first in the set (as in []abc]) is a member, and
              so is a - first or last
       [^...] any character not in the set ([^]...] likewise)
       *      zero or more of the preceding item
       ^      at the start of the pattern: the beginning of a line
       $      at the end of the pattern: the end of a line

       Quote patterns that hold * [ ? $ or spaces from the shell.

OPTIONS
       Options must come before the pattern; they may be combined, as
       in -vn. -- ends the options, so that a pattern may begin
       with -. An unknown option is a usage error.

       -v     print the lines that do not match
       -n     precede each line with its line number and a colon
       -c     print only the number of selected lines (per file, as
              name:count, when there are several files)
       -i     ignore the case of letters

EXAMPLES
       grep root /etc/passwd
       grep -n 'printf' *.c          where printf is used
       grep -c '^$' notes            count the empty lines
       grep -v '^#' /etc/rc          the rc without its comments
       ps -f | grep ken

EXIT STATUS
       0 if any line was selected, 1 if none was, 2 for a usage
       error or if any file could not be opened (grep says why and
       goes on with the next). An error gives 2 even when lines
       were selected from the other files, as POSIX asks.

SEE ALSO
       ed(1), vi(1), find(1), sh(1)

BUGS
       There are no -e, -f, -l, -h, -s, -w, -x, -E or -F options,
       and no +, ?, | or \( \) in patterns (no egrep or fgrep).
